Aiming to refute a <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/08/10/all-glass-20th-anniversary-iphone-canceled/">report from Jefferies analyst Edison Lee</a> earlier today that claimed Apple's all-glass iPhone planned for the device's 20th anniversary next year has been canceled, <em>Bloomberg</em>'s Mark Gurman says that the <a href="https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2026-08-11/apple-s-glass-centric-20th-anniversary-iphone-remains-on-track-for-2027">"glass-centric" redesign remains on track for 2027</a>.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/07/iphone-20-anniversary.jpg" alt="" width="1672" height="941" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1057094" /><br/>
Gurman says that the 20th-anniversary iPhone will make heavy use of glass, with the front and rear wrapping around the edges to sandwich a thin metal band.<blockquote>The company expects to launch iPhone Pro models next year that will make use of a new glassy look, according to the people, who asked not to be identified because the work is confidential. Glass is used on the front and back of the phones — known internally as V73 and V74 — and the material will curve into the sides of the devices, with a metal band in the middle.</blockquote>Gurman does mention a "more aspirational version" that "used even more glass," but says that design "was scrapped early on" in the development cycle.<br/>
<br/>
It's possible that this more ambitious design is the one Lee was referring to in today's report, but Gurman implies that it was discarded some time ago, rather than being a more recent development as Lee had suggested.<br/>
<br/>
